, then lost 8.0 points with D3, a solid connection on the third line. Black never got back from there. White's one big mistake, F7, lost 4.7, but Black's reply lost 4.5 of its own, so the gap stayed put.", "moments": [{"turn": 13, "text": "C5 took Black's lead from +0.2 to -3.4, a 3.7-point loss, with H4 as the engine's choice. D4 two turns later lost 4.2. The left-side fight started drifting away from GPT-6.1 Sol here."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "White's C8 lost 3.9 points and handed the lead back from -3.9 to 0.0. After this move the game was dead level, and Black only had to find a good reply."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "D3 was the decisive move. It closed the gap in Black's row-3 wall and dropped the lead from 0.0 to -8.0, an 8.0-point loss. The engine preferred H3. Black got a tidy wall, but it lost the game here."}, {"turn": 25, "text": "G7 went in at -7.8 and left Black at -12.2, a 4.5-point loss. The engine wanted J4. White's F7 answer lost 4.7 and pulled the lead back to -7.5, so the mistake cost nothing in the end."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "H7 repeated the pattern: it lost 4.6 and sent the lead from -7.6 to -12.3. G8, F8, H8 and E9 each lost 4.1 to 4.2, and the engine asked for J4 every time. White answered each one with a move that lost about as much, so the margin stayed near -8 to -12."}, {"turn": 41, "text": "J3 finally came to the right corner and lost 2.1, from -7.7 to -9.8. White took J4 at no cost, and the game ran out through the passes at about 10 points down."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "The `inspect` function stored in call 4 only parses the board text, lists chains and liberties, and applies one named move to report captures and the resulting chain. The `play` wrapper just sends the move and logs it, and the call 62 replay and SGF save only reconstruct the actual game record. Nothing looped over empty points to score candidates or played continuations forward.", "stop": false}}